Topic: Client-Side Scripting, DOM Manipulation, and Event Handling in JavaScript
Client-side scripting is code—almost always JavaScript—that runs inside the user's web browser after the web page has been downloaded, rather than on the web server.It enables interactive web features such as:
Validating form inputs before submission
Updating page content dynamically
Animating elements
Responding to user clicks and keystrokes instantly without page reloads HTML: Defines the Structure of the web page.
CSS: Defines the Presentation and styling.
JavaScript: Controls the Behavior and interactivity.
The Document Object Model (DOM) is the browser's live, in-memory object representation of an HTML document. When a browser loads HTML, it converts every tag into an object arranged in a hierarchical tree structure. document | &lt;html&gt; / \ &lt;head&gt; &lt;body&gt; / | \ &lt;h1&gt; &lt;p&gt; &lt;button&gt; | "Hello!" Document Node: The root node representing the entire web page (document).
Element Nodes: HTML tags like &lt;html&gt;, &lt;body&gt;, &lt;h1&gt;, &lt;p&gt;, &lt;button&gt;.
Container Nodes: Elements containing nested tags.
Text Nodes: The actual text content inside tags (e.g., "Hello!").
Key Concept
HTML is what the server sends once. The DOM is what the browser maintains and updates live. Modifying the DOM instantly updates what is displayed on the screen.
Before JavaScript can modify or read an element, it must first locate it in the DOM tree. document.getElementById("idName") Description: Finds the single element with the specified ID.
Returns: A single Element object, or null if not found.
Best for: Fast, direct selection when an ID is known. document.querySelector("cssSelector") Description: Finds the first element that matches any CSS selector (e.g., .class, #id, tag).
Returns: The first matching Element, or null. document.querySelectorAll("cssSelector") Description: Finds all elements matching the given CSS selector.
Returns: A NodeList (a list-like collection of elements). // Selecting elements by ID, class, and query selector
const mainTitle = document.getElementById("main-title");
const primaryBtn = document.querySelector(".btn");
const allNotes = document.querySelectorAll(".note"); // Checking selections in the browser DevTools console
console.log(mainTitle);
JavaScript provides two main properties to alter element content:
textContent: Gets or sets plain text. It does not parse HTML tags, making it fast and safe from cross-site scripting (XSS) attacks.
innerHTML: Gets or sets HTML markup inside an element. It parses HTML tags.
Security Caution
Avoid assigning un-sanitized user inputs directly to innerHTML as it opens vulnerability vectors for Cross-Site Scripting (XSS). Use textContent for plain text.
&lt;!-- HTML --&gt;
&lt;h1 id="title"&gt;Old Heading&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;div id="box"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
// JavaScript
const title = document.getElementById("title");
const box = document.getElementById("box"); // Safely updating text
title.textContent = "Welcome Back!"; // Updating with HTML markup
box.innerHTML = "&lt;strong&gt;Loaded&lt;/strong&gt; successfully";
You can dynamically update an element's inline styles, attributes, or CSS classes.&lt;!-- HTML --&gt;
&lt;p id="msg"&gt;Status&lt;/p&gt; /* CSS */
.active { color: green; font-weight: bold;
}
// JavaScript
const msg = document.getElementById("msg"); // Add CSS class and set hover attribute
msg.classList.add("active");
msg.setAttribute("title", "Saved successfully");
JavaScript allows you to build new DOM elements from scratch and remove existing ones.
document.createElement("tagName"): Instantiates a new element in memory.
parentElement.appendChild(childElement): Appends the element as the last child of a parent.
element.remove(): Deletes an element directly from the DOM tree.
&lt;!-- HTML --&gt;
&lt;ul id="list"&gt; &lt;li id="temp"&gt;Temporary Item&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
// JavaScript
const list = document.getElementById("list"); // 1. Create a new &lt;li&gt; element
const newLi = document.createElement("li");
newLi.textContent = "Fresh Milk"; // 2. Append it to the list container
list.appendChild(newLi); // 3. Remove an old element
const tempItem = document.getElementById("temp");
tempItem.remove();
An event is a signal sent by the browser indicating that an action has occurred—such as a user clicking a button, typing in an input field, scrolling the page, or the page finishing loading.
User Action: User clicks, types, or scrolls.
Event Fires: The browser emits an event signal (e.g., "click").
Listener Catches It: addEventListener() intercepts the event.
Handler Runs: The associated callback function executes.
Modern JavaScript uses addEventListener() to bind event handlers to DOM elements.// Syntax
element.addEventListener("eventType", function(event) { // Handler code executed when event triggers
}); Separates JavaScript behavior from HTML markup.
Allows attaching multiple event listeners to the same element without overwriting existing ones.
&lt;!-- HTML --&gt;
&lt;button id="likeBtn"&gt;♡ Like&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;p id="count"&gt;0 likes&lt;/p&gt;
// JavaScript
const btn = document.getElementById("likeBtn");
const count = document.getElementById("count"); let likes = 0; btn.addEventListener("click", () =&gt; { likes++; count.textContent = `${likes} likes`;
});
Tip
Always pass event names as plain lower-case strings without the "on" prefix (e.g., "click", "submit", "input").
When an event handler runs, the browser automatically passes an Event object (e or event) containing details about what happened.
e.target: References the exact element where the event originated.
e.preventDefault(): Cancels default browser behavior (such as page reloads on form submission or link navigation).
&lt;!-- HTML --&gt;
&lt;form id="signup"&gt; &lt;input type="email" id="email" placeholder="Enter email" /&gt; &lt;button type="submit"&gt;Sign Up&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;/form&gt;
// JavaScript
const form = document.getElementById("signup"); form.addEventListener("submit", (e) =&gt; { // Prevent the default browser page reload e.preventDefault(); console.log("Form target element:", e.target); alert("Thanks for signing up!");
});
Combining element selection, event handling, DOM element creation, and resetting values:&lt;!-- HTML --&gt;
&lt;input id="taskInput" placeholder="Enter new task..." /&gt;
&lt;button id="addBtn"&gt;Add Task&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;ul id="taskList"&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;
// JavaScript
const input = document.getElementById("taskInput");
const addBtn = document.getElementById("addBtn");
const list = document.getElementById("taskList"); addBtn.addEventListener("click", () =&gt; { // 1. Validate input (ignore empty or whitespace-only text) if (input.value.trim() === "") return; // 2. Create new &lt;li&gt; element const li = document.createElement("li"); li.textContent = input.value; // 3. Append to list list.appendChild(li); // 4. Reset input field input.value = "";
}); Select: Input, button, and list references are saved upfront.
Listen: Click event attached to the button.
Validate: trim() checks for empty inputs.
Create: New &lt;li&gt; node built with text from input.
Insert: appendChild() puts the new item at the end of &lt;ul&gt;.
Reset: Input field cleared for next entry. Structure HTML: Create markup and assign clean, descriptive id or class attributes to interactive elements.
Link Script: Embed script with &lt;script src="app.js" defer&gt;&lt;/script&gt; before &lt;/body&gt; or in &lt;head&gt; with defer.
Select Elements: At top of JavaScript file, query DOM elements.
Attach Event Listeners: Register addEventListener() for user actions.
Manipulate DOM: Inside event callbacks, update text, styles, or add/remove nodes.
Test &amp; Debug: Use Browser Developer Tools (F12) Console and Elements panels.
A modal is a dialog box sitting on top of page content over a backdrop. Instead of creating complex elements on the fly, modals are usually pre-built in HTML/CSS and toggled using CSS classes via JavaScript.&lt;!-- Modal HTML Structure --&gt;
&lt;button id="openBtn"&gt;Open Modal&lt;/button&gt; &lt;div class="modal-overlay" id="myModal"&gt; &lt;div class="modal"&gt; &lt;button class="close"&gt;&amp;times;&lt;/button&gt; &lt;h2&gt;Modal Title&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;Modal content goes here...&lt;/p&gt; &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
/* Modal CSS Styles */
.modal-overlay { display: none; /* Hidden by default */ position: fixed; inset: 0; background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5);
} .modal-overlay.open { display: flex; /* Displayed when 'open' class is added */ align-items: center; justify-content: center;
}
const openBtn = document.getElementById("openBtn");
const modal = document.getElementById("myModal");
const closeBtn = modal.querySelector(".close"); // 1. Open modal on button click
openBtn.addEventListener("click", () =&gt; { modal.classList.add("open");
}); // 2. Close modal on close button click
closeBtn.addEventListener("click", () =&gt; { modal.classList.remove("open");
}); // 3. Close modal when clicking the backdrop overlay
modal.addEventListener("click", (e) =&gt; { if (e.target === modal) { modal.classList.remove("open"); }
}); // 4. Close modal on Escape key press
document.addEventListener("keydown", (e) =&gt; { if (e.key === "Escape") { modal.classList.remove("open"); }
}); modal.querySelector(".close"): Scopes the search specifically inside the modal container.
e.target === modal: Ensures the modal closes only when clicking the backdrop overlay directly, not when clicking inside the content box.
e.key === "Escape": Listens on the global document so pressing Escape works regardless of where keyboard focus currently sits.
classList.add() / classList.remove(): Explicitly manages visual state cleanly. Client-Side Scripting runs in the browser, providing instant interactive feedback without server requests.
The DOM is a dynamic live object tree representing your page HTML.
Select elements with getElementById() or querySelector().
Modify content using textContent (plain text) or innerHTML (HTML markup).
Style elements with style, setAttribute(), or classList.
Create/Delete elements using createElement(), appendChild(), and remove().
Handle Events with element.addEventListener("type", handler) (avoid inline HTML handlers).
Build interactive UI components (modals, dropdowns, tooltips) by toggling CSS state classes in response to user actions.

