its-300/subnotes/media-types.html.description:
Media queries (@media) in CSS3 allow you to apply specific styles based on a device's characteristics—such as screen width, height, orientation, or user preferences like dark mode. They are the foundation of responsive web design, ensuring your site looks great on smartphones, tablets, and desktop displays alike.Before @media queries will work properly on mobile devices, you must include this &lt;meta&gt; tag inside your HTML &lt;head&gt; section:&lt;meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0"&gt;
Note
Without this, mobile browsers will pretend they are desktop browsers and zoom out, breaking your responsive layouts.
A media query consists of a media type, optional logical operators, and media features inside parentheses:@media media-type and (media-feature) { /* CSS rules go here */
} Media Types: screen (computer/phone screens), print (print preview/paper), all (default for all devices).
Media Features: min-width, max-width, orientation, prefers-color-scheme, etc.
Write base styles for mobile screens first, then use min-width queries to add or adjust styles as the screen gets wider./* Base styles (Mobile) */
.container { width: 100%; padding: 10px;
} /* Tablet and larger (768px and up) */
@media screen and (min-width: 768px) { .container { width: 80%; padding: 20px; }
} /* Desktop and larger (1024px and up) */
@media screen and (min-width: 1024px) { .container { width: 1200px; margin: 0 auto; }
}
Write base styles for large screens first, then use max-width queries to adjust styles as the viewport shrinks./* Base styles (Desktop) */
.sidebar { width: 300px; float: left;
} /* Mobile (767px or smaller) */
@media screen and (max-width: 767px) { .sidebar { width: 100%; float: none; }
}
While exact breakpoints depend on your design, these industry standards are a great starting point:
Use and to combine rules:
/* Applies only if screen is between 600px and 900px */
@media screen and (min-width: 600px) and (max-width: 900px) { body { background-color: lightblue; }
} Use a comma , to act as an OR operator:
/* Applies if screen is in landscape OR width is at least 1200px */
@media (orientation: landscape), (min-width: 1200px) { .hero { height: 100vh; }
} Dark Mode Preference:
@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) { body { background-color: #121212; color: #ffffff; }
} Print Styles:
@media print { .no-print { display: none; } body { color: #000; }
}

